  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 AM EDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nHurricane Isaias made landfall and is moving through the Florida \nPanhandle into southern Alabama. Its remnants will then progress \nnorth to NNW through the day, eventually tracking more northeast \nthrough Tennessee into Kentucky by Sunday. Within the past hours, \nhigh elevation observations have already confirmed wind gusts of \nover 35 mph with the 850mb flow exceeding 60 kts by the morning and \nremaining similarly high through the day. This will promote mountain \nwave enhanced winds for the mountains and foothills, along with \nbreezy conditions for the rest of the area. The only change to the \nexisting wind products was the addition of Cherokee and Clay \nCounties as high-res guidance does indicate surface winds reaching \n60 mph or more, especially in Clay County. It is unclear how much \nwill reach the lower elevations due to mixing and downsloping, but, \nbut synoptic-scale winds and the MSLP gradient will remain strong \narea-wide. Winds will gradually decrease tonight as the flow weakens \nand becomes more southerly.\n\nRegarding the rainfall, downsloping will limit rainfall totals in \nour eastern areas with the heavier totals expected in western and \nsouthern areas. Upsloping in North Carolina will produce much \nheavier totals, which could influence river levels, especially to \nour east. Otherwise, the flooding threat will be limited to more of \nour western and southern areas. Regarding the threat for convection, \ninstability will be limited and elevated, keeping the severe threat \nto our south, but this could bring down more of the 850mb winds. The \nheaviest of the rain will fall from Saturday afternoon through the \nfirst half of Sunday night with diminishing coverage on Sunday. \n\nAs the remnants of Isaias lift to the north and east Sunday night \ninto Monday, ridging and high pressure will build in, leading to \ndrier conditions to start the week. As ridging expands from the \nsouth, a warming trend will take place with much of the region well \ninto the 80s by mid-week. Another system may develop and track to \nour north during the second half of the week, which could bring a \nreturn of rain chances in the north.",
